02,2493,15170,16,17,92,488,1124,15940,1124,37018,90,18,59,2493,15170,16,17,92,488,1124,15940,1124,37018,90,20,59,2493,15170,16,17,92,1124,1291,340,59,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nCompute:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12}.\n\\]\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the given problem, we need to compute the sum of the sines of the angles \\(\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\), \\(\\frac{3\\pi}{12}\\), \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\), \\(\\frac{7\\pi}{12}\\), \\(\\frac{9\\pi}{12}\\), and \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{12}\\). Let's denote this sum by \\(S\\):\n\n\\[\nS = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we use the identity \\(\\sin(\\pi - x) = \\sin x\\). This allows us to pair the terms in the sum as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{3\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\n\\]\n\nSo the sum \\(S\\) can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\nS = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\right) + \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{3\\pi}{12}\\right) + \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{12}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nS = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}\n\\]\n\\[\nS = 2 \\left( \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} \\right)\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b9d8947a42f93aebb4f9dbddf8099ad7d2429861a6921ad70b2d9a056f701304"},"seconds":70.7806146144867,"seed":769270697646158976,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem of computing the sum of sines:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12},\n\\]\n\nwe can use symmetry properties of the sine function and some trigonometric identities.\n\n1. **Symmetry and Simplification:**\n   Utilize the identity \\(\\sin(\\pi - x) = \\sin x\\). This will allow us to pair terms effectively:\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{3\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Rewriting the Sum:**\n   Using the above identities, we can rewrite the original sum \\(S\\) as:\n   \\[\n   S = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}.\n   \\]\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   S = 2 \\left( \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} \\right).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Evaluating the Sine Values:**\n   Calculate each sine value:\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} = \\sin 15^\\circ,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\sin 45^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} = \\sin 75^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n   We know:\n   \\[\n   \\sin 15^\\circ = \\sin (45^\\circ - 30^\\circ) = \\sin 45^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ - \\cos 45^\\circ \\sin 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin 75^\\circ = \\sin (45^\\circ + 30^\\circ) = \\sin 45^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ + \\cos 45^\\circ \\sin 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Summing the Values:**\n   Substitute back into the sum:\n   \\[\n   S = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right).\n   \\]\n   Combine the terms inside the parentheses:\n   \\[\n   S = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2} + 2\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = 2 \\left( \\frac{2\\sqrt{6} + 2\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}.\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the problem of computing the sum of sines:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12},\n\\]\n\nwe can use symmetry properties of the sine function and some trigonometric identities.\n\n1. **Symmetry and Simplification:**\n   Utilize the identity \\(\\sin(\\pi - x) = \\sin x\\). This will allow us to pair terms effectively:\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{3\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Rewriting the Sum:**\n   Using the above identities, we can rewrite the original sum \\(S\\) as:\n   \\[\n   S = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}.\n   \\]\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   S = 2 \\left( \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} \\right).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Evaluating the Sine Values:**\n   Calculate each sine value:\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} = \\sin 15^\\circ,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\sin 45^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} = \\sin 75^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n   We know:\n   \\[\n   \\sin 15^\\circ = \\sin (45^\\circ - 30^\\circ) = \\sin 45^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ - \\cos 45^\\circ \\sin 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin 75^\\circ = \\sin (45^\\circ + 30^\\circ) = \\sin 45^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ + \\cos 45^\\circ \\sin 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Summing the Values:**\n   Substitute back into the sum:\n   \\[\n   S = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right).\n   \\]\n   Combine the terms inside the parentheses:\n   \\[\n   S = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2} + 2\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = 2 \\left( \\frac{2\\sqrt{6} + 2\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}.\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}\n\\]","id":"precalculus:416"}
